            <div xml:id="d630">

                <pb facs="0673" n="575" sameAs="frus:frus1932v04/pg575"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/5528: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China (Johnson) to the
                            Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-09-06T16:00:00+08:00">September 6, 1932—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received September 6—6:50 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1073. Department’s 301, September 2, 6 p.m. Colonel Gulick made no protest to
                    Japanese. I do not know where John
                        Goette got incorrect information which he cabled. Colonel Gulick called on Japanese
                    to request they discontinue holding maneuvers in private compound of one of the
                    officers of our own guard. Legation here is doing all it can to avoid situation
                    described in last paragraph Department’s telegram.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
